<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luanda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1576">1576</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as São Paulo de Loanda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luanda is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, on the Atlantic coast. It was founded in 1575–76 by the Portuguese captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulo-dias-de-novais">Paulo Dias de Novais</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> de Loanda and became the centre of Portuguese Angola and one of the busiest slave-trading ports in the Atlantic world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> held it from 1641 to 1648, and it became the capital of independent Angola in 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luanda.jpg" alt="Luanda" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iamdelcioborges · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luanda_2025_focused_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Luanda lies on the northern Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and is the country's chief seaport and its administrative, industrial and cultural centre. It has a hot, semi-arid climate, unusually dry for its tropical latitude because the cool Benguela Current keeps moisture from condensing into rain. The old city, the Baixa de Luanda, stands beside the port with narrow streets and colonial buildings, and the Cidade Alta is the newer part.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulo-dias-de-novais">Paulo Dias de Novais</a> founded the city on 25 January 1576 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> da Assumpção de Loanda, bringing one hundred families of settlers and four hundred soldiers. The Portuguese built the Fortaleza São Pedro da Barra in 1618 and the Fortaleza de São Miguel in 1634, and the city was their bridgehead in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> from 1627, apart from a period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule, as Fort Aardenburgh, that ended in 1648.</p><p>Luanda was the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> until the trade was abolished in 1836. Brazilian ships were the most numerous in its port, and local merchants and warriors profited; in 1751 between 5,000 and 10,000 slaves were sold there each year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>'s ports were opened to foreign shipping in 1844, and by 1850 Luanda was one of the most developed Portuguese cities outside Portugal, exporting palm and peanut oil, wax, copal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>. It grew from 61,208 inhabitants in 1940 to 475,328 in 1970.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> on 25 April 1974, independence in 1975 and the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angolan-civil-war">Angolan Civil War</a>, most white Portuguese residents left as refugees. Migrants fleeing the war swelled the population, and slums called musseques spread far beyond the old limits. The war ended in 2002, and major reconstruction began.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Luanda is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and holds about a third of its population. Portuguese is the official language, and Kimbundu, Umbundu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongo-language">Kikongo</a> are also spoken. It is the seat of a Roman Catholic archbishop and the home of most of Angola's universities. Surviving monuments include the Fortress of São Miguel, the best preserved of the colonial forts, Luanda Cathedral and the Palácio de Ferro, and the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> is in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luanda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom was also a major producer and exporter of cloth, to the interior and to the Portuguese at <b>Luanda</b>, where thousands of metres of Loango cloth were imported in the early seventeenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The ‘20. and odd’ Africans landed in English Virginia in August 1619 were probably captives from Portuguese campaigns against Ndongo, shipped from <b>Luanda</b> on a slave ship bound for Veracruz.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ndongo">Kingdom of Ndongo · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The growth of the Atlantic slave trade aggravated internal conflicts, as rival factions competed for control of trade routes and access to European goods, while the founding of Portuguese Angola at <b>Luanda</b> in 1576 created a hostile neighbour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kongo-empire">Kongo Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1640 to 1648 <b>Luanda</b> was Dutch, ruled as Fort Aardenburgh: the Dutch, who had seized north-eastern Brazil, wanted slaves for its sugarcane plantations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese and later Brazilian merchants became its principal organisers, drawing captives above all from West Central Africa through <b>Luanda</b> and other ports.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Dutch–Portuguese war, a Dutch West India Company fleet under Cornelis Jol captured <b>Luanda</b> in August 1641, aiming to secure enslaved labour for the sugar plantations of Dutch Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-angola">Dutch Angola</a></li></ul></section>
